In Python, a constructor is a special method used to initialize (set up) an object when a class object is created.
🔹 What is a Constructor?
•	A constructor runs automatically when you create an object
•	It is defined using __init__()
•	Used to initialize instance variables
🔹 Syntax of Constructor
class ClassName:
    def __init__(self):
        # initialization code
🔹 Example: Simple Constructor
class Person:
    def __init__(self):
        print("Constructor called")

p1 = Person()
📌 Output:
Constructor called
🔹 Constructor with Parameters
class Person:
    def __init__(self, name, age):
        self.name = name
        self.age = age

    def display(self):
        print("Name:", self.name)
        print("Age:", self.age)
p1 = Person("Anirudha", 25)
p1.display()
📌 Output:
Name: Anirudha
Age: 25
🔹 Why use self in Constructor?
•	self refers to the current object
•	It stores values inside the object
•	Each object gets its own copy
🔹 Types of Constructors in Python
1️ Default Constructor
(No parameters except self)
class Demo:
    def __init__(self):
        self.x = 10
2️ Parameterized Constructor
class Demo:
    def __init__(self, x, y):
        self.x = x
        self.y = y
🔹 Constructor Overloading?
Python does not support multiple constructors directly.
❌ This will not work:
def __init__(self):
def __init__(self, x):
✅ Use default arguments instead:
class Demo:
    def __init__(self, x=0):
        self.x = x
🔹 Constructor vs Normal Method
Constructor (__init__)	Normal Method
Runs automatically	Called manually
Initializes object	Performs actions
Name is fixed	Any name

🔹 Real-life Example (Bank Account)
class BankAccount:
    def __init__(self, name, balance):
        self.name = name
        self.balance = balance

    def deposit(self, amount):
        self.balance += amount

    def show_balance(self):
        print("Balance:", self.balance)
        
🔹 Key Points to Remember
✔ Constructor name is __init__
✔ Called once per object creation
✔ Used to initialize object data
✔ Uses self keyword

